WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:13.170
Hi! When the lights go off and the room is dark, we can't see anything. Why do we need light to see? And where do shadows come from? Today let's learn about light.

2
00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:18.810
The Sun, a lamp and a fire give out their own light. They are light sources.

3
00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:24.306
A book does not give out light. Light from a light source shines on the book,

4
00:00:24.456 --> 00:00:35.058
and the book reflects the light into our eyes, so we see the book. To see an object, either it gives out light, or it reflects light into our eyes.

5
00:00:35.208 --> 00:00:40.242
Light travels in straight lines. It does not bend round corners.

6
00:00:40.392 --> 00:00:47.370
So when an opaque object blocks the light, the light can't reach the space behind it, and a shadow forms.

7
00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:58.338
A translucent object, like tracing paper, blocks only part of the light, so its shadow is lighter. Clear glass lets most light through, so there is almost no shadow.

8
00:00:58.488 --> 00:01:06.066
Move the object nearer the torch and it blocks more light, so the shadow on the screen becomes bigger.

9
00:01:06.216 --> 00:01:11.370
Watch out! The Moon looks bright at night, but it is not a light source!

10
00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:17.442
The Moon gives out no light of its own. It reflects light from the Sun into our eyes.

11
00:01:17.592 --> 00:01:24.666
A mirror is the same: it looks bright because it reflects light, but it is not a light source.

12
00:01:24.816 --> 00:01:34.986
Your turn! The Sun, the Moon, a lamp and a mirror: which are light sources? Pause and think.

13
00:01:35.136 --> 00:01:42.786
The Sun and the lamp. The Moon and the mirror only reflect light. Did you get it right?

14
00:01:42.936 --> 00:01:53.370
Another one! To make the shadow on the screen smaller, which way should we move the object? Pause and think.

15
00:01:53.520 --> 00:02:02.682
Move it away from the torch, nearer the screen. It blocks less light, so the shadow gets smaller. Did you get it right?

16
00:02:02.832 --> 00:02:15.234
Next one! A wooden board and a sheet of tracing paper are held in front of a torch. Which makes the darker shadow? Pause and think.

17
00:02:15.384 --> 00:02:28.458
The wooden board. It is opaque and blocks all the light, so its shadow is dark. Tracing paper is translucent and lets some light through, so its shadow is lighter. Did you get it right?

18
00:02:28.608 --> 00:02:37.890
Last one! Why does a shadow have the same outline as the object? Pause and think.

19
00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:47.874
Because light travels in straight lines and can't bend round behind the object, the blocked part has the object's shape. Did you get it right?

20
00:02:48.024 --> 00:03:02.538
Remember: we see an object when it gives out light or reflects light into our eyes. Light travels in straight lines, so an opaque object that blocks light makes a shadow. The nearer the object is to the light, the bigger the shadow.

21
00:03:02.688 --> 00:03:08.874
Tonight, use a torch and your hands to make a shadow animal on the wall!

